The trio, verified:

```js
const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

nums.map(n => n * 10);         // [10,20,30,40,50,60]  — 1:1 transform
nums.filter(n => n % 2 === 0); // [2,4,6]             — keep the true- predicates
nums.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);  // 21            — fold to ONE value

// reduce builds ANYTHING — group by:
const words = ['apple', 'avocado', 'banana', 'cherry'];
words.reduce((groups, w) => {
  const key = w[0];
  (groups[key] ||= []).push(w);    // logical-OR assign: init if absent
  return groups;
}, {});   // { a: ['apple','avocado'], b: ['banana'], c: ['cherry'] }
```

The signature contracts (the details that matter):
- map(fn): fn's return BECOMES the element — return undefined and you get holes of undefined
- filter(fn): fn returns a BOOLEAN-ish — TRUTHY keeps, falsy drops (return the VALUE itself for keep-and-transform: filter(u => u.active && u))
- reduce(fn, initial): without the initial value, the FIRST element is the accumulator's start — and reduce on an EMPTY array throws TypeError ('Reduce of empty array with no initial value'). ALWAYS pass the initial — it's the type-safe contract.

Chained vs single-pass (the performance judgment):
```js
// 3 passes, 2 intermediate arrays — clear but allocation-heavy:
users.filter(u => u.active).map(u => u.name).slice(0, 10);

// 1 pass, no intermediates — the same result:
users.reduce((acc, u) => u.active && acc.length < 10 ? [...acc, u.name] : acc, []);
// Rule of thumb: chain for readability on normal-sized data;
// single pass when arrays are 10k+ or in a hot loop.
```

